TI  - Use of p-31 magnetic resonance spectroscopy to detect metabolic abnormalities in muscles of patients with fibromyalgia. lane
PY  - 1998
T2  - Radiology
VL  - 208
IS  - 1
SP  - 277
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/33748192183?origin=resultslist
AB  - Objective: To investigate the metabolic and functional status of muscles of fibromyalgia patients by using phosphorus-31 magnetic resonance (MR) spectroscopy. Methods: Twelve patients with fibromyalgia and 11 healthy subjects were studied. Clinical status was assessed with a questionnaire. The biochemical status of muscle was evaluated with P-31 MR spectroscopy by determining concentrations of inorganic phosphate (Pi), phosphocreatine (PCr), adenosine triphosphate (ATI'), and phosphodiesters during rest and exercise. Functional status was evaluated from the PCr/Pi ratio, phosphorylation potential (PP), and total oxidative capacity (VnMx). Results: Patients with fibromyalgia reported greater difficulty in performing activities of daily living as well as increased pain, fatigue, and weakness compared with controls. MR spectroscopic measurements showed that patients had significantly lower than normal PCr and ATP levels (/' < .004) and I'Cr/I'i ratios (/' < .04) in the quadriceps muscles during rest. Values for PP and V were also suhstantially reduced during rest and exercise. Conclusion: P-31 MR spectroscopy provides objective evidence for metabolic abnormalities consistent with weakness and fatigue in patients with fibromyalgia. It may be useful in assessing clinical status and evaluating the effectiveness of treatment regimens in fibromyalgia. Authors' Abstract.
